|\n56-| 8 | Narrowness | Kornai & Tuza 1992 [11] | graph | itself | `ν = pw + 1` for ≥ 1 vertex (Prop. 3.1); per sequence `ν(σ) = vs(σ reversed) + 1`; in- = out-narrowness (Prop. 2.1) | 1,652 graphs, 0 fail | `Complex.narrowness_eq_pathwidth_add_one`, `inNarrowness_eq_vertexSepOfLayout_reverse`, `exists_inNarrowness_iff_exists_outNarrowness` | confirmed; **proved** (needs ≥ 1 vertex; `ν = 0` on the empty graph) |\n57-| 9 | Split bandwidth | Fomin 1998 [12] | connected graph, ≥ 2 vertices (Lean: every finite graph) | itself | **sandwich** `pw ≤ sb ≤ pw + 1` (Thm 8), not an equality; `sb = ib = 1/μ_m` (Thms 3, 6) | 1,302 graphs via `ib`; `ib − pw ∈ {0,1}` | `Complex.pathwidth_le_splitBandwidth_le_pathwidth_add_one`, `pathwidth_le_splitBandwidth`, `splitBandwidth_le_pathwidth_add_one`, `pathwidth_le_of_isNodeSplitting`, `splitBandwidth_le_inNarrowness` | weaker than stated (sandwich); **proved** (no hypothesis) |\n58-| 10 | Graph path-width | Kinnersley 1992 [13] | graph | itself | definition (Robertson & Seymour), p. 346 | reference | `Pathwidth.lean` | definition |\n59-| 11 | Edge separation | Lengauer 1981 [14] | graph | itself | [14] defines no edge-separation number related to `pw`: its edge game is min-cut linear arrangement (cutwidth, p. 468), its Def. 6 is modified cutwidth; neither is within ±1 (stars); its vertex game VSG is `vs` exactly | `cw`, `mcw` fail ±1 on 341, 738 of 1,644 | `Complex.vsg_eq_pathwidth`, `vsg_eq_one_of_edgeless`, `vertexSeparation_triangleGraph` (Thm 4), `isPositiveVSG_iff_triangleGraph`; counterexamples `pathwidth_add_two_lt_cutwidth_star7`, `pathwidth_add_two_lt_modCutwidth_star9`, `cutwidth_unbounded`, `modCutwidth_unbounded`; `pathwidth_le_cutwidth` | **misattributed and false** under every reading [14] supports; **proved**: VSG = vs = pw (≥ 1 edge; VSG = 1 edgeless), Thm 4, and the star counterexamples |\n60-| 12 | Vertex separation | Kinnersley 1992 [13] | graph | itself | `vs = pw` (Thm 3.1); Lengauer's VSG `= vs` by reversal | 1,652 graphs, 0 fail | `vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth`; VSG: `Complex.vsg_eq_vertexSeparation` | confirmed |\n61-\n62-Summary: **six confirmed** (MOSP, gate matrix layout, one-dimensional logic,\n63:narrowness, node search with the edgeless exception, vertex separation), plus\n64-path-width as the reference definition; **one unsourced** at its citation but\n65-proved elsewhere (interval thickness); **two weaker than stated** (edge search,\n66-a band; split bandwidth, a sandwich), both of which still meet the literal ±1\n67-wording; **two false as stated** (PLA folding in its standard two-nets-per-track\n68-form; edge separation, which is also misattributed).\n69-\n70-## The census, problem by problem\n71-\n72-### 1. MOSP — [1] Yanasse 1997 (EJOR), [4] Fink & Voss 1999\n73-\n74-- **Definition.** [1] p. 455: \"We assume that a stack is opened for every new\n75-  panel type cut and it remains opened until the last piece of that panel type\n76-  is cut. We also assume that a completed stack can be removed only after a\n77-  pattern is completely cut, hence, the maximum number of stacks occurs just\n78-  after some pattern is cut and before any completed part stack is removed.\"\n79-  [4] §1.1.1 (preprint p. 2), with `C` an m × n matrix whose **rows are\n80-  patterns** and columns order types: \"an order j may be defined open at\n81-  position i₀ of the pattern sequence if (Σ_{i ≤ i₀} c_{π_i,j})(Σ_{i ≥ i₀}\n82-  c_{π_i,j}) > 0\". Linhares & Yanasse 2002, p. 1760, eqs. (1)–(2), with the\n83-  I × J matrix P whose **rows are piece types**: `q_ij = 1` iff some patterns\n84-  x, y containing piece i satisfy `π(x) ≤ j ≤ π(y)`, and `Z_MOSP(P) = min_π\n85-  max_j Σ_i q_ij`. The three agree up to transposition of the matrix.\n86-- **Input.** 0/1 matrix. **Graph.** The MOSP graph: piece types (customers),\n87-  adjacent iff some pattern contains both (Yanasse 1997a Prop. 5).\n88-- **Relation.** Neither [1] nor [4] relates MOSP to any graph parameter; [1]\n89-  relates it to tool switching (Props. 1–2). The relation `Z = pw + 1` is\n90-  Yanasse 1997a Prop. 5 (the MOSP graph) with L&Y 2002 Prop. 2 (MOSP = GMLP)\n91-  and Fellows & Langston 1989 Thm 7 (GMLP cost k ⇔ pathwidth k − 1), whose\n92-  sketch rests on F&L 1987 Lemma 4.1. Proved in Lean,\n93-  `mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one`, hypothesis: at least one requirement\n94-  (with none, `Z = 0` while `pw + 1 = 1`).\n95-- **Status.** Confirmed. The Table 1 references are sources for the problem,\n96-  not for the equivalence.\n97-\n98-### 2. Gate matrix layout — [6] Möhring 1990, [8] Wing, Huang & Wang 1985\n99-\n100-- **Definition.** Möhring p. 18: an m × n net–gate matrix M, rows nets,\n101-  columns gates; for a gate permutation π the augmented matrix M^π fills in\n102-  every 0 between a row's leftmost and rightmost 1; \"Nets of the augmented\n103-  net-gate matrix may share the same row (called track) if they have no gate\n--\n222-  in [6] and follows from [9] + [10].\n223-\n224:### 6. Node search game — [9] Kirousis & Papadimitriou 1985\n225-\n226-- **Definition.** [9] p. 181: \"A searching strategy S is a sequence of moves\n227-  where the player either places a searcher on a node of the graph that\n228-  carries no searcher or deletes the searcher of a guarded node. The edges of\n229-  the graph are initially considered contaminated by a gas. The object of a\n230-  searching strategy is to clear all edges. The clearing of an edge is\n231-  accomplished once both its endpoints concurrently carry a searcher. A clear\n232-  edge may be recontaminated once there appears a path that carries no\n233-  searchers and that connects this edge with a contaminated one.\" ns(G): the\n234-  least maximum number of searchers (p. 182). Same in [10] §2 p. 208.\n235-- **Relation.** [9] Theorem (p. 182): \"For any graph G, ns(G) = θ(G).\" Its\n236-  proof of `θ ≤ ns` takes an optimal *recontamination-free* strategy, which\n237-  exists by [10] Thm 2.3 (`pns = ns`), itself derived from LaPaugh's theorem\n238-  for edge search ([10] Thm 2.1). [10] Thm 4.1 (p. 216): \"For an arbitrary\n239-  graph G, ns(G) = vs(G) + 1\"; its `vs ≤ ns − 1` direction also uses an\n240-  optimal recontamination-free strategy. The Lemma of [9] (p. 182): a strategy\n241-  in which each node takes a searcher once and loses it only after all its\n242-  neighbours have taken one is recontamination-free.\n243-- **Which paper proves what** (item 01's question): **[9] (1985) proves\n244:  interval thickness = node search number; [10] (1986) proves node search =\n245:  vs + 1** (Thm 4.1), monotonicity of node search (Thm 2.3), `es(G) =\n246-  ns(G_v) − 1` for the three-edge subdivision `G_v` (Thm 2.5), and `mpb = ns =\n247:  mpbw` (Thm 3.1). Table 1's pairing of node search with [9] is right.\n248-- **Edge case.** On an edgeless graph there is nothing to clear, so by the\n249-  letter of the definition `ns = 0`, while `vs + 1 = θ = 1` (with ≥ 1 vertex).\n250-  Both theorems hold for every graph with at least one edge (then `ns ≥ 2`).\n251-  Möhring's Thm 3.9 (p. 34) restates [9] with the same gap.\n252-- **Status.** Confirmed, for graphs with at least one edge. The monotone game\n253-  gives both equalities by elementary arguments (items 10, 12); the full game\n254-  needs monotonicity (item 14).\n255-\n256-### 7. Edge search game — [10] Kirousis & Papadimitriou 1986\n257-\n258-- **Definition.** [10] §2 p. 208 (after Megiddo et al.; Parsons 1976): moves\n259-  are sliding a searcher along an edge, placing a searcher, deleting a\n260-  searcher; \"An edge with at least one guarded endpoint is cleared by sliding\n261-  along it a searcher, and then placing this searcher on the unguarded\n262-  endpoint ... If all other edges incident on the first guarded vertex are\n263-  clear, the guard itself may be used for the sliding\"; recontamination by\n264-  unguarded paths; es(G) the least maximum number of searchers. EST 1994 p. 53\n265-  gives the same game, and allows multigraphs and loops, which can change es\n266-  but not vs (p. 50).\n267-- **Relation.** [10] p. 209 (unnumbered, proved in the text): \"for any graph\n268-  G, ns(G) − 1 ≤ es(G) ≤ ns(G) + 1\", with examples of all three cases (Fig. 1:\n269-  es 1, ns 2; Fig. 2: 2, 2; Fig. 3: es 5, ns 4). With Thm 4.1 this is `vs ≤ es\n270-  ≤ vs + 2`, stated in [10] p. 216 as due to Turner, and proved as EST 1994\n271-  Thm 2.1 (p. 54) from Lemma 2.1 (`vs ≤ s`, which uses LaPaugh's theorem) and\n272-  Lemma 2.2 (`s ≤ vs + 2`, the procedure `search1`). Tightness: K_{3,3} has\n273-  vs 3, s 5 (EST p. 57, via cutwidth for max degree 3), and there is a tree\n274-  with the same difference (EST §3.3, Fig. 3.6). Exact under a transformation:\n275-  EST Thm 2.2, `s(G) = vs(G')` with G' the 2-expansion (each edge subdivided\n276-  twice).\n277-- **Status.** Weaker than stated. `es ∈ {pw, pw + 1, pw + 2} = {Z − 1, Z, Z + 1}`\n278-  meets the literal ±1 wording; it is not a fixed offset (K_2: es = 1 = Z − 1;\n279-  K_{3,3}: es = 5 = Z + 1).\n280-\n281-### 8. Narrowness — [11] Kornai & Tuza 1992\n282-\n283-- **Definition.** §2 (preprint p. 2): for an in-sequence `(v_1, …, v_n)`, \"In\n284-  the i-th step, put vertex v_i from the IM into the shack; move all v_j (j ≤\n285-  i) with no neighbors v_k, k > i, from the shack to the OM; then go to the\n286-  (i+1)-st step. The maximum number of vertices in the shack during this\n287-  process ... will be called the narrowness of the in-sequence\". \"Definition.\n--\n408-   graph of the chromatic (= clique) number (Thm 3.2), = max column sum of\n409-   the augmented matrix under the best permutation (p. 31), = `pw + 1`\n410:   (Prop. 3.5, proved), = node search number (Thm 3.9, citing [9]). PLA\n411-   folding (at most two nets per track): `|V| − s` for a maximum folding set\n412-   (Prop. 3.15, citing [HNS82]), equivalent to a maximum Z_{m,m} subgraph\n413-   (Prop. 3.16), NP-hard (Thms 4.5–4.6); **no ±1 relation** to `pw`. Multiple\n414-   folding (path partitions) is equivale