{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfZg64Vn6gGcqk4Upiuvc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I finished item 10: `lean/MOSPFormalization/Complex/NodeSearch.lean` proves that monotone node search equals vertex separation + 1 on every graph with at least one edge. It has no `sorry`, uses only the standard axioms (`propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`), is imported from the root file, and the gate passes (lake build, sorry count unchanged at 1, 1,231 tests).\n\n**How the game is defined.** It follows Kirousis & Papadimitriou (1985), p. 181:\n- A move places a searcher on a node or removes one.\n- After each move, every edge whose two endpoints both carry a searcher becomes clear.\n- Contamination then spreads back onto any clear edge that a searcher-free path joins to a contaminated one.\n- A monotone strategy is one where no move enlarges the contaminated set.\n\nThese are the same rules as the checker's `node_search`, and nothing in the definitions mentions layouts.\n\n**What is proved:**\n- **Main result:** `monotoneNodeSearch_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one`, which is Theorem 4.1 of the 1986 paper for the monotone game. With the existing vertex separation = pathwidth result, this gives `monotoneNodeSearch_eq_pathwidth_add_one`.\n  - Upper bound: Kornai & Tuza's shack process from item 04, played as searcher moves, never recontaminates, clears every edge, and uses exactly the narrowness ν(σ) searchers.\n  - Lower bound: order the vertices by the time from which no contaminated edge touches them.\n- **Full game (non-monotone), one direction:** node search ≤ vertex separation + 1.\n- **Edgeless graphs:** the search number is 0 (the empty strategy clears everything), so the equality really does fail there.\n\n**Remaining gap.** The full game's other direction (node search ≥ vertex separation + 1) needs Theorem 2.3 of the 1986 paper: recontamination does not help. I did not prove it. It is written as a proposition, `NodeSearchMonotonicity`, that is stated but not assumed anywhere. The full Theorem 4.1 is proved conditional on it. Since no `sorry` stands for it, `allowed_sorries.txt` is unchanged.\n\n**A gap in the 1986 paper's proof (p. 217).** The paper orders vertices by when each first receives a searcher. Its claim (2) fails for monotone strategies that place a searcher and remove it before clearing anything. Example on K₁,₃: place and remove each leaf in turn, then search from the centre. The strategy is monotone and uses 2 searchers, but the paper's count is 3 against its bound of 1. The theorem itself still holds, and the Lean proof avoids the problem by ordering by clearing time. I checked this example by hand only, not in Lean.\n\n**Docs and loop files.** `paper2/equivalences.md` has row 6 of the master table updated and a new \"Item 10\" section. In `PROGRESS.md`, iteration 10 is appended and the count is now 10/14 SOLVED. Item 10 is marked `[x]` in `iterations.md`. Nothing was committed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2417,"cache_read_input_tokens":163573,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2417,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":764,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"223159c0-79bb-4af4-9a3b-463f27701b36","uuid":"61de7d26-d3f3-4a25-b39c-b5304847ff55","timestamp":"2026-09-30T13:07:13.620Z","request_id":"req_011CfZg643jodpCXfsEbXoWo"}
